### What is a Service Registry?

A Service Registry is a runtime database that stores the current list of service instances, their network addresses, and their health. Every time a microservice instance starts, it registers itself by telling the registry its logical service name, its host and port, and often metadata like version or zone. It then sends periodic heartbeats to prove it is alive.

```java
package com.bank.discovery;

import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.cloud.netflix.eureka.server.EnableEurekaServer;

@SpringBootApplication
@EnableEurekaServer
public class RegistryServerApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(RegistryServerApplication.class, args);
        System.out.println("Service Registry started on port 8761");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Service Registry started on port 8761
```

Examples of registries include Netflix Eureka, HashiCorp Consul, Apache ZooKeeper, etcd, and the built-in mechanism behind Kubernetes Services.

### What is Service Discovery?

Service Discovery is the process of resolving a logical service name into a concrete, healthy network address at runtime. It is the activity that consumes the registry's data. Without discovery, a caller would need to hardcode downstream addresses, which breaks the moment instances scale, restart, or move.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.cloud.client.ServiceInstance;
import org.springframework.cloud.client.discovery.DiscoveryClient;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class TransferService {

    private final DiscoveryClient discoveryClient;

    public TransferService(DiscoveryClient discoveryClient) {
        this.discoveryClient = discoveryClient;
    }

    public String getPaymentServiceUrl() {
        List<ServiceInstance> instances = discoveryClient.getInstances("payment-service");
        if (instances.isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("No healthy payment-service instance found");
        }
        ServiceInstance instance = instances.get(0);
        return instance.getUri().toString();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
http://10.0.3.14:8080
```

### Why do we need both?

In microservices, instances are dynamic: they scale up during month-end salary processing at National Bank of India, scale down afterward, restart on new hosts, and fail unexpectedly. Hardcoding addresses is impossible because addresses change constantly. The registry provides the live directory, and discovery provides the lookup mechanism. One is the data store; the other is the query.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.cloud.client.discovery.EnableDiscoveryClient;

@SpringBootApplication
@EnableDiscoveryClient
public class PaymentServiceApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(PaymentServiceApplication.class, args);
        // This instance registers itself with the registry on startup
        System.out.println("Payment Service registered with Eureka");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Payment Service registered with Eureka
```

### How does client-side discovery differ from server-side discovery?

In client-side discovery, the calling service queries the registry, gets the list of healthy instances, and picks one using a load-balancing rule. Spring Cloud LoadBalancer works this way. In server-side discovery, the client calls a stable address like a load balancer or a Kubernetes Service, and that intermediary resolves the name.

| Aspect | Client-Side Discovery | Server-Side Discovery |
|---|---|---|
| Who queries the registry | The calling service itself | A load balancer or gateway |
| Load-balancing logic | Inside the caller | Inside the intermediary |
| Example tools | Spring Cloud LoadBalancer, Ribbon | Kubernetes Service, AWS ALB |
| Caller complexity | Higher, carries discovery logic | Lower, just calls a stable address |
| Network hops | Fewer, direct call to instance | One extra hop through the intermediary |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class AccountService {

    private final WebClient.Builder webClientBuilder;

    public AccountService(WebClient.Builder webClientBuilder) {
        this.webClientBuilder = webClientBuilder;
    }

    public String settleTransfer(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Client-side discovery: "lb://" triggers the load balancer to resolve
        return webClientBuilder.build()
                .post()
                .uri("lb://payment-service/api/v1/settle")
                .bodyValue(new SettleRequest(accountId, amount))
                .retrieve()
                .bodyToMono(String.class)
                .block();
    }

    record SettleRequest(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {}
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Settlement confirmed for account SAV2001
```

### How do health checks keep the registry accurate?

Health checks are what prevent discovery from handing out dead addresses. A liveness check answers whether the process is alive, and a readiness check answers whether it can accept traffic. A service that is alive but still warming its connection pool should report not-ready so no requests are routed to it.

```yaml
# application.yml for a payment-service instance
eureka:
  instance:
    lease-renewal-interval-in-seconds: 10
    lease-expiration-duration-in-seconds: 30
  client:
    service-url:
      defaultZone: http://localhost:8761/eureka/
```

```java
package com.bank.health;

import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.Health;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.HealthIndicator;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class DatabaseHealthIndicator implements HealthIndicator {

    @Override
    public Health health() {
        boolean dbReachable = checkDatabaseConnection();
        if (dbReachable) {
            return Health.up().withDetail("database", "National Bank of India core DB reachable").build();
        }
        return Health.down().withDetail("database", "Core DB unreachable").build();
    }

    private boolean checkDatabaseConnection() {
        // Simulated check
        return true;
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
{"status":"UP","details":{"database":"National Bank of India core DB reachable"}}
```

### What happens when an instance dies?

When an instance crashes, it stops sending heartbeats. After a configured number of missed heartbeats, the registry evicts the entry. Discovery then stops returning that address. There is a small staleness window between the crash and the eviction, which is why discovery is always combined with retries and circuit breakers.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.annotation.CircuitBreaker;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class ResilientTransferService {

    private final WebClient.Builder webClientBuilder;

    public ResilientTransferService(WebClient.Builder webClientBuilder) {
        this.webClientBuilder = webClientBuilder;
    }

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "payment", fallbackMethod = "fallbackSettle")
    public String settle(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        return webClientBuilder.build()
                .post()
                .uri("lb://payment-service/api/v1/settle")
                .bodyValue(new SettleRequest(accountId, amount))
                .retrieve()
                .bodyToMono(String.class)
                .block();
    }

    public String fallbackSettle(String accountId, BigDecimal amount, Throwable t) {
        return "Settlement queued for retry: " + accountId;
    }

    record SettleRequest(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {}
}
```

```text
Expected output (when payment-service is down):
Settlement queued for retry: SAV2001
```

### How does the API Gateway use discovery?

The API Gateway is a consumer of discovery. It resolves backend services by their logical names so it can route incoming requests. Spring Cloud Gateway integrates with Eureka and uses the `lb://` scheme to resolve routes dynamically.

```yaml
# Gateway route configuration
spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      routes:
        - id: payment-route
          uri: lb://payment-service
          predicates:
            - Path=/api/v1/payments/**
        - id: account-route
          uri: lb://account-service
          predicates:
            - Path=/api/v1/accounts/**
```

```text
Gateway resolves payment-service to 10.0.3.14:8080 via Eureka
Gateway resolves account-service to 10.0.3.15:8081 via Eureka
```

### What consistency trade-offs do registries make?

Registries like Eureka favor availability over consistency (AP in the CAP theorem). When a network partition occurs, Eureka continues serving the last known list rather than refusing, which is the right choice for discovery because approximate freshness combined with retries is better than no answer at all. Consul and etcd lean toward consistency (CP), which suits different use cases like configuration storage.

| Registry | Consistency Model | Best For |
|---|---|---|
| Eureka | AP (available, partition-tolerant) | Service discovery where staleness is tolerable |
| Consul | CP (consistent, partition-tolerant) | Discovery plus configuration and KV storage |
| etcd | CP (strongly consistent) | Kubernetes backing store, leader election |
| Kubernetes DNS | Server-side, built-in | Pod-to-pod discovery inside a cluster |

```java
package com.bank.config;

// Eureka self-preservation mode: if too many instances miss heartbeats at once,
// Eureka assumes a network partition rather than evicting them all.
// This is an AP trade-off: better to serve stale data than no data.

// eureka.server.enable-self-preservation=true  (default)
// eureka.server.renewal-percent-threshold=0.85
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer understands that the registry is a trust anchor. Registration should be authenticated to prevent registry poisoning, where a rogue instance registers and hijacks traffic. In production, combine discovery with mutual TLS so the caller also verifies the instance's identity. Monitor registry size, heartbeat rates, and eviction counts as operational metrics.

```java
package com.bank.metrics;

import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import org.springframework.cloud.client.discovery.DiscoveryClient;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class DiscoveryMetrics {

    private final DiscoveryClient discoveryClient;
    private final MeterRegistry meterRegistry;

    public DiscoveryMetrics(DiscoveryClient discoveryClient, MeterRegistry meterRegistry) {
        this.discoveryClient = discoveryClient;
        this.meterRegistry = meterRegistry;
    }

    @Scheduled(fixedRate = 30000)
    public void recordInstanceCounts() {
        int paymentInstances = discoveryClient.getInstances("payment-service").size();
        meterRegistry.gauge("discovery.instances.payment", paymentInstances);
        System.out.println("Payment service instances: " + paymentInstances);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Payment service instances: 3
```
